/// @mainpage Focus Framework
///
/// Focus is a lightweight C++ UI framework for resource-constrained devices. It provides a view hierarchy with layout, event dispatch, touch and directional input, focus management, and text rendering — all in a cooperative single-threaded model with no OS dependencies beyond a display backend.
///
/// @section modules Modules
///
/// - @ref core — Application lifecycle, view hierarchy, event dispatch, and display.
/// - @ref controls — Interactive UI elements: buttons, text fields, sliders, checkboxes.
/// - @ref views — Concrete view implementations for layout, display, and content.
/// - @ref viewcontrollers — Container view controllers for navigation and modal presentation.
/// - @ref text — Font loading, glyph rendering, text layout, and Unicode support.
/// - @ref settings — Platform-agnostic key-value settings with pluggable backends.
/// - @ref locale — Localized string tables with placeholder substitution and plurals.
///
/// @section getting_started Getting Started
///
/// A minimal Focus application creates a Display backend, a Window, and an Application subclass:
///
/// @code
/// class MyApp : public Application {
/// public:
///     MyApp(const std::shared_ptr<Window>& window) : Application(window) {}
///     void setup() override {
///         auto rootVC = std::make_shared<MyViewController>(this->shared_from_this());
///         this->setRootViewController(rootVC);
///     }
/// };
///
/// // In main():
/// auto display = std::make_shared<MyDisplay>(480, 800);
/// auto window = std::make_shared<Window>(display, MakeSize(480, 800));
/// auto app = std::make_shared<MyApp>(window);
/// app->run();  // enters the cooperative run loop
/// @endcode
///
/// @section threading Threading Model
///
/// Focus is single-threaded cooperative. All event dispatch, layout, drawing, and callbacks execute on the thread that calls Application::run(). There is no internal locking. If your application needs to communicate with Focus from another thread (e.g., a network callback), post a notification or enqueue a DeferredTask from the main thread.
///
/// @section memory Memory Management
///
/// Views are reference-counted via std::shared_ptr. The view hierarchy holds strong references from parent to child; back-references (superview, window) are raw pointers or weak_ptr. Font and Locale caches grow without bound; call Font::clearCache() and Locale::clearCache() from your platform's memory-pressure handler to reclaim memory. See the Font and Locale class documentation for details.
